(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antivirals-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antivirals Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two conditions does varicella-zoster virus cause?","choices":["Retinitis and colitis","Infectious mononucleosis and hepatitis","Chickenpox and shingles","Cold sores and genital ulceration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — primary infection and later reactivation of the same virus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What route and dose adjustment does oseltamivir need?","choices":["Available orally only, and requiring renal dose adjustment","Available intravenously only, with hepatic adjustment","Available orally and intravenously, with no adjustment needed","Available as an inhaled powder only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renal adjustment is a consideration rather than a dose to memorise. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"Which renal effect does acyclovir cause, and how is it prevented?","choices":["Its intravenous vehicle accumulates in renal failure","It crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration and renal function must be maintained","It causes direct renal tubule damage requiring hydration with normal saline","It causes interstitial nephritis requiring corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Note how this differs from amphotericin B, which damages the tubule directly rather than crystallising in it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach acts on the host rather than the virus?","choices":["Stimulating the host immune system","Inhibiting specific viral enzymes","Blocking viral assembly","Inhibiting viral protein synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is the only one of the eight aimed at the host side of the interaction. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"What is oseltamivir's mechanism of action?","choices":["Inhibits neuraminidase so progeny cannot be released","It blocks viral attachment to the host cell receptor","It is a guanine analogue that terminates the viral nucleic acid chain","Inhibits a viral protease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it blocks viral release rather than viral entry or replication. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Herpes simplex meningitis. Which type affects the meninges?","choices":["Neither type; meningitis is a cytomegalovirus manifestation","Neither type; meningitis is caused by varicella-zoster only","Type 2","Type 1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges; type 1 covers mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does the acyclovir family represent?","choices":["Blocking uncoating after entry","Inhibiting viral assembly","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Inhibiting viral release"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the drug is incorporated into viral DNA and terminates the chain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Which non-neurologic adverse effects does acyclovir cause?","choices":["Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol","Nausea, vomiting, rash and bone marrow suppression","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia as the dominant effects","Photosensitivity and tooth discoloration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with hydration and renal function to be maintained because of tubular crystallisation. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What does acyclovir achieve if started within 24 hours of chickenpox?","choices":["It shortens the acute illness but does not cure the infection","It cures the infection and prevents later reactivation as shingles","It has no effect unless combined with valacyclovir","It prevents transmission to contacts but does not alter the illness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the virus is not eradicated, which is why reactivation as shingles remains possible later. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"How is acyclovir selective for infected cells?","choices":["Viral thymidine kinase activates it only in infected cells","A transporter on infected cells concentrates it","It binds a viral capsid protein that has no human counterpart","It is activated only at the low pH found inside infected cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Selectivity comes from an enzyme the virus supplies, which is the cleanest form of selective toxicity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Once in viral DNA, how does acyclovir stop replication?","choices":["It causes helical structure loss and strand breakage","It inhibits DNA gyrase and prevents supercoiling","It is a guanine analogue lacking the sugar moiety, so the chain cannot elongate past it","It cross-links the two strands of viral DNA so that they can no longer separate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chain termination by a missing sugar, which is a structural rather than an enzymatic block. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Which antiviral prodrug and parent pairing is correct?","choices":["Oseltamivir-acyclovir, valacyclovir-penciclovir, famciclovir-ganciclovir","There are no antiviral prodrugs","Valacyclovir-acyclovir, famciclovir-penciclovir, valganciclovir-ganciclovir","Valacyclovir-ganciclovir, famciclovir-acyclovir, valganciclovir-penciclovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In each case the prodrug exists to solve poor oral absorption of the parent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"Which is the better-absorbed oral form, ganciclovir or valganciclovir?","choices":["Ganciclovir, the parent compound","Both are equally absorbed","Neither is absorbed orally; both are intravenous only","Valganciclovir, the prodrug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ganciclovir's oral bioavailability is very low, which is why the prodrug exists. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which blood effects limit ganciclovir?","choices":["Megaloblastic anaemia and agranulocytosis from folate antagonism","Haemolysis triggered by glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Isolated eosinophilia with no clinical consequence","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and about a third of patients must stop intravenous treatment because of side effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which enzyme does acyclovir need, and which does oseltamivir target?","choices":["Acyclovir hits neuraminidase; oseltamivir needs thymidine kinase","Both depend on viral thymidine kinase for activation","Both target viral neuraminidase at different sites","Acyclovir needs thymidine kinase; oseltamivir hits neuraminidase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one uses a viral enzyme to switch itself on, the other shuts a viral enzyme off. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"Why is renal function watched on acyclovir?","choices":["It causes interstitial nephritis as a hypersensitivity reaction","The drug crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained","The drug is nephrotoxic through direct tubular necrosis","Its vehicle accumulates in renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a physical rather than a toxic mechanism of renal injury. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"Which viruses does oseltamivir target?","choices":["Cytomegalovirus in transplant recipients","Influenza A and B, in a season running roughly October to March","Influenza A only, year-round","Herpes simplex and varicella-zoster, circulating year-round"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and resistance has been becoming a problem over time. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which sites does herpes simplex type 1 affect?","choices":["Genitals, rectum, hands or meninges","Retina, oesophagus and colon","Skin in a dermatomal distribution following chickenpox","Mouth, face, skin, oesophagus or brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type 2 covers genitals, rectum, hands or meninges — the split is anatomical rather than absolute. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"Why is ganciclovir harder to tolerate than acyclovir?","choices":["A disulfiram-like reaction on any alcohol exposure","Neutropenia and thrombocytopenia; a third stop treatment","Crystals in the renal tubule; hydrate throughout","Severe infusion reactions requiring premedication"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acyclovir's bone marrow suppression is listed, but ganciclovir's is far more limiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Why is acyclovir wrong for cytomegalovirus retinitis?","choices":["Acyclovir is intravenous only and unsafe in transplant","Acyclovir causes neutropenia in a third of patients","Acyclovir has no antiviral activity at all in immunocompromised patients","Acyclovir for simplex and zoster; ganciclovir for CMV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All are guanine analogues, but the indications are drawn along virus lines rather than by drug family. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"}]};
